.TH SSH\-SELECTOR 1 "2026-10-06" "mu2edaq-cluster-tools 1.0.1" "Mu2e DAQ Manual"
.SH NAME
ssh\-selector \- terminal UI for picking and connecting to SSH hosts
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ssh\-selector
.RB [ \-c
.IR FILE ]
.br
.B ssh\-selector
.BR \-\-version | \-h
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ssh\-selector
is a Textual terminal UI over a YAML host list.
Hosts are shown in named groups, two per row, and can be filtered by
nickname or hostname.
Each host may have several login users, a non-default port and a ProxyJump
host.
The tool builds and runs
.BR ssh (1)
command lines; it holds no credentials and reads no
.I ~/.ssh/config
of its own, although
.B ssh
does.
.PP
The right-hand panel shows the highlighted host's details and the
.B ssh
command for its first user, the output of
.BR klist (1)
refreshed every 60 s with expiry colouring (green over 2 h, yellow under,
red expired), the local hostname, IP and IPv4 subnets, and the background
tunnels started in this session.
.PP
At start-up every hostname is resolved with
.BR gethostbyname (3);
the dot next to a host shows whether the name resolved, not whether the host
answers.
If a host has a ProxyJump and its resolved address lies inside one of the
local IPv4 subnets, the jump is skipped.
.PP
Choosing a host (Enter) leaves the UI and runs
.B ssh
in the current terminal; when the session ends the UI comes back.
.BR ssh 's
own standard error is captured during the session and shown in a dialog if
it exits non-zero, so its warnings are not visible while the session runs.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " \fIFILE\fR"
YAML host file.
Overrides
.B SSH_SELECTOR_CONFIG
and skips discovery.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print
.B ssh\-selector
.I version
and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help and exit.
.SH CONFIG FILE DISCOVERY
The host file is chosen by the first of these that applies:
.IP 1. 4
.B \-c
.IR FILE .
.IP 2. 4
.BR SSH_SELECTOR_CONFIG ,
a file path; a leading
.I ~
is expanded.
.IP 3. 4
Discovery.
Directories are searched in tiers; the first tier holding any
.I *.yaml
file supplies all candidates and later tiers are not consulted:
.RS
.IP a. 3
.I ./
and
.I ./config/
(relative to the current directory, merged in that order);
.IP b. 3
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/mu2edaq/cluster\-tools/ ,
by default
.IR ~/.config/mu2edaq/cluster\-tools/ ;
.IP c. 3
.IR <install\ dir>/config/ ,
where the install directory is
.B MU2EDAQ_CLUSTER_TOOLS_INSTALL_DIR
if set, else the parent of the running venv when it carries the
.BR install.sh (1)
marker file
.IR .mu2edaq\-cluster\-tools\-install .
.RE
.PP
Duplicates by real path are dropped.
One candidate is loaded directly; several are offered in a picker; none is an
error that lists the directories searched.
A file named by
.B \-c
or
.B SSH_SELECTOR_CONFIG
that does not exist is an error; there is no fallback to discovery.
.PP
.I *.yml
and
.I *.yaml.example
files are not candidates.
Any other
.I *.yaml
file in a searched directory is, so keep non-host YAML (for example
.B lan\-scan
profiles) out of them; such a file stops with "No hosts defined".
.SH CONFIGURATION
Top-level keys:
.TP
.B hosts
Required list.
Each entry needs
.BR hostname ;
optional
.B nickname
(default the hostname),
.BR group ,
.B port
(default 22),
.B users
or
.B user
(a list or a scalar; the first is the default login),
.B proxy_jump
and
.BR proxy_user .
.TP
.B grouplist
Display order of group names.
Groups used by hosts but not listed follow in order of first use; hosts with no
group appear under "Other".
.TP
.B groups
Per-group defaults:
.BR users ,
.BR proxy_jump ,
.BR proxy_user .
A host key that is present, even as
.BR null ,
overrides the group value.
.TP
.B cache_lifetime
Seconds a "Check Users" result stays fresh.
Default 600.
.TP
.B panel_width
Width of the right-hand panel in columns.
Default 50.
.PP
The user
.B default
means the current login name
.RB ( $USER ).
The jump user is
.B proxy_user
when set, else the destination user, unless
.B proxy_jump
already contains
.BR user@ .
The resulting commands are
.PP
.RS
.nf
ssh [\-p PORT] [\-J JUMPUSER@JUMPHOST] \-l USER HOST
ssh \-N \-L LOCAL:localhost:REMOTE [\-p PORT] [\-J ...] \-l USER HOST
.fi
.RE
.PP
.I settings.md
in the source tree is the full reference;
.I config/hosts.yaml.example
is an annotated example.
.SH KEY BINDINGS
.TP
.B Enter
Connect in the current terminal.
A user picker appears when the host has more than one user.
.TP
.B n
Connect in a new terminal window: Terminal.app via
.BR osascript (1)
on macOS; otherwise the first of
.BR gnome\-terminal ,
.BR xfce4\-terminal ,
.BR konsole ,
.B xterm
found on
.BR PATH .
The host list is then rebuilt, as after a foreground session.
.TP
.B t
Port-forward tunnel.
Prompts for a local and a remote port (1\-65535) and a background switch
(default on).
In the background the tunnel is a child
.B ssh \-N
process listed in the Tunnels panel; in the foreground the UI exits, the
tunnel runs until Ctrl-C, and the UI comes back.
.TP
.B c
"Check Users": runs
.B ssh \-o BatchMode=yes \-o ConnectTimeout=5 ... who
against
.I every
host in the file at once, as each host's first user and through its jump host,
and shows the number of lines
.B who
prints (12 s limit per host).
Results are reused for
.B cache_lifetime
seconds.
There is no limit on concurrent connections.
.TP
.B /
Focus the filter field.
.TP
.B r
Run
.B kinit \-R
(renew the current ticket) and refresh the panel.
.TP
.B A
About screen.
.TP
.B q
Quit.
.TP
.B Left\fR, \fBRight
Move between the two hosts of a row.
.TP
.B Escape
In the filter field, clear it and return to the list; elsewhere, quit.
In a dialog, cancel it.
.PP
In the Tunnels panel,
.B k
or
.B Delete
sends SIGTERM to the highlighted background tunnel.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ./*.yaml\fR, \fI./config/*.yaml
Discovered host files, first tier.
.TP
.I ~/.config/mu2edaq/cluster\-tools/*.yaml
Per-user host files, second tier
(under
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME
when set).
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/mu2edaq\-cluster\-tools/config/*.yaml
Topology files copied by
.BR install.sh (1)
(default install directory), third tier.
.TP
.I config/mu2e_daq.yaml
Mu2e DAQ topology (75 hosts in 13 groups).
.TP
.I config/nova_daq.yaml\fR, \fIconfig/mu2e_offline.yaml\fR, \fIconfig/fnal_gpmv.yaml
NOvA DAQ, Mu2e offline and Fermilab GPVM host lists.
.TP
.I config/hosts.yaml.example\fR, \fIconfig/example.yaml
Annotated examples.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SSH_SELECTOR_CONFIG
Host file to load when
.B \-c
is not given.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Base of the per-user config directory (default
.IR ~/.config ).
.TP
.B MU2EDAQ_CLUSTER_TOOLS_INSTALL_DIR
Install directory whose
.I config/
is the last discovery tier; also read by
.BR install.sh (1).
.TP
.B USER
Login name used for the
.B default
user (falls back to
.BR getpass.getuser ()).
.TP
.B KRB5CCNAME
Not read directly; honoured by
.BR klist ,
.B kinit
and
.BR ssh .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Quit from the host list or the config picker.
.TP
.B 1
No config file found, the
.B \-c
or
.B SSH_SELECTOR_CONFIG
file does not exist, the file fails to load, or it defines no hosts.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command line.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
ssh\-selector \-c config/mu2e_daq.yaml
cd ~/clusters && ssh\-selector        # pick among ./*.yaml
SSH_SELECTOR_CONFIG=~/hosts.yaml ssh\-selector
mkdir \-p ~/.config/mu2edaq/cluster\-tools && cp hosts.yaml ~/.config/mu2edaq/cluster\-tools/
.fi
.RE
.SH BUGS
Background tunnels belong to one run of the UI.
After Enter, n or a foreground t the UI is rebuilt with an empty Tunnels panel,
and on quit the tunnels keep running; neither case stops them.
They are started without
.BR "ExitOnForwardFailure=yes" ,
so a new tunnel whose local port is still held by an old one stays up,
forwards nothing, and is listed as alive.
.PP
.B xfce4\-terminal \-e
expects one string but is given the command as separate words.
On Windows the new-terminal path calls
.BR which ,
which does not exist there.
.SH KERBEROS SUPPORT
Ticket display and renewal need
.B klist
and
.B kinit
on
.BR PATH .
On Windows this works within WSL and from PowerShell when Kerberos is
configured; MIT Kerberos for Windows has compatibility issues with some
terminal emulators.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR lan\-scan (1),
.BR ssh (1),
.BR klist (1),
.BR kinit (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Andrew Norman, Mu2e DAQ Group, Fermi National Accelerator Laboratory.
